I am a PhD candidate at the Department of Political Science at the University of Salzburg, supervised by [Robert A. Huber](https://robertahuber.com/), [Federica Genovese](https://www.federica-genovese.com/), and [Diane Bolet](https://www.dianebolet.com/). In 2026, I was a Visiting Scholar at Nuffield College, University of Oxford, and at the Department of Social Policy at the London School of Economics and Political Science. 

My research examines the drivers of support for populist radical right parties, with a particular focus on party strategies, structural economic change, and distributive conflicts. 

Specifically, I study how the green transition and the expansion of AI infrastructure can create place-based grievances and reshape electoral coalitions, how radical right parties mobilize these grievances, and whether policy design can mitigate the resulting backlash. Moreover, I analyze whether mainstream party strategies are effective. Methodologically, I employ causal inference designs and computational text analysis.

My work has been published or accepted in *Electoral Studies* and the *Journal of European Public Policy*.